# Landflow — UI / shadcn Rule

This rule defines visual and component implementation standards for Landflow.

Primary references:

- `docs/product/PRODUCT.md`
- `docs/product/WEBFLOW_TO_LANDFLOW.md`
- `docs/architecture/BLOCK_SYSTEM.md`
- `docs/automation/DEFINITION_OF_DONE.md`
- `.cursor/rules/00-project-core.mdc`
- `.cursor/rules/30-react-inertia.mdc`

Landflow should feel like one coherent professional SaaS product.

---

## 1. UI Foundation

Use:

- shadcn/ui
- Tailwind CSS 4
- existing project utilities
- existing icon system

Do not introduce another component framework.

Do not rebuild standard primitives from scratch without a clear product reason.

---

## 2. shadcn Is the Default Primitive Layer

Prefer shadcn for:

- Button
- Input
- Textarea
- Select
- Checkbox
- Radio
- Switch
- Dialog
- AlertDialog
- Drawer/Sheet
- Popover
- Dropdown Menu
- Tabs
- Tooltip
- Table
- Badge
- Card
- Separator
- Skeleton
- Command
- ScrollArea
- Breadcrumb
- Form-related primitives

If a suitable shadcn component exists, start there.

---

## 3. Product Components Are Compositions

Build Landflow-specific components by composing primitives.

Examples:

```text
SiteCard
WorkspaceSwitcher
PublishDialog
VehicleCard
PriceEditor
BlockPropertiesPanel
IntegrationProfileCard
SubmissionStatusBadge
```

Do not create a second internal primitive library that duplicates shadcn.

---

## 4. Existing Utility Conventions

Use the existing:

```text
@/lib/utils
```

and existing `cn()` utility.

Do not create:

- `classNames.ts`
- another `cn`
- duplicate styling helpers

without a concrete reason.

---

## 5. Radix Policy

Use granular Radix packages required by shadcn.

Do not add the umbrella `radix-ui` package merely for convenience.

Avoid duplicate overlapping primitive packages.

---

## 6. User Interface Language

All product UI is Russian.

This includes:

- labels;
- buttons;
- placeholders;
- menus;
- tabs;
- breadcrumbs;
- tooltips;
- notifications;
- empty states;
- errors;
- dialogs;
- tables;
- filters;
- onboarding;
- admin UI;
- developer UI;
- marketplace UI.

Code identifiers remain English.

---

## 7. Russian Copy Must Fit

Russian text is often longer than English.

Design components to survive longer labels.

Check:

- buttons;
- sidebar;
- tabs;
- dialog footer;
- table headers;
- filter chips;
- dropdown options;
- breadcrumbs;
- mobile layouts.

Do not design around short temporary English labels.

---

## 8. Product Tone

UI copy should be:

- concise;
- professional;
- clear;
- neutral;
- action-oriented.

Prefer:

`Создать сайт`

over:

`Начать процесс создания нового сайта`

Prefer:

`Не удалось сохранить изменения`

over:

`Произошла неизвестная ошибка при попытке выполнения операции сохранения`

---

## 9. Visual Consistency

Do not create unique styling rules for every page.

Maintain consistency in:

- spacing;
- radii;
- typography;
- form height;
- button hierarchy;
- cards;
- page headers;
- dialogs;
- empty states.

A user should immediately recognize every surface as Landflow.

---

## 10. Design System Layers

Think in layers:

```text
Primitive
→ Shared UI Pattern
→ Product Component
→ Feature Screen
```

Example:

```text
Button
→ ConfirmationFooter
→ DeleteSiteDialog
→ Site Settings
```

Avoid skipping straight from raw HTML to one-off page design.

---

## 11. Layout System

Pages should use consistent layout conventions.

Typical authenticated screen:

```text
App Shell
├── Sidebar / navigation
├── Top/header area
└── Main content
    ├── Page header
    ├── actions
    └── content
```

Do not invent a completely different layout for every feature.

---

## 12. Page Header Pattern

A normal management page should usually have:

- title;
- optional description;
- primary action;
- optional secondary actions.

Example:

```text
Сайты
Управляйте сайтами рабочего пространства.
[Создать сайт]
```

---

## 13. Primary Action Hierarchy

Each screen should normally have one visually dominant primary action.

Examples:

- `Создать сайт`
- `Добавить автомобиль`
- `Опубликовать`
- `Сохранить`

Do not make several unrelated buttons look equally primary.

---

## 14. Button Variants

Use shadcn variants consistently.

Typical meaning:

- default → primary action;
- secondary → secondary action;
- outline → neutral alternative;
- ghost → lightweight/contextual;
- destructive → destructive operation.

Do not encode semantic meaning with random custom colors.

---

## 15. Destructive Actions

Destructive actions must be clear.

Examples:

- удалить сайт;
- удалить участника;
- отвязать домен;
- архивировать интеграцию.

Use destructive styling and confirmation when consequences matter.

---

## 16. Form Field Layout

Forms should use consistent field structure:

```text
Label
Control
Optional description/help
Validation error
```

Do not rely on placeholder as the only label.

---

## 17. Form Width

Do not stretch short forms across an entire wide screen without reason.

Use readable widths.

Settings forms usually benefit from constrained content width.

Complex Designer panels are an exception.

---

## 18. Input Heights

Use consistent input/button heights throughout product surfaces.

Do not mix many random sizes unless hierarchy requires it.

---

## 19. Required Fields

Required state should be understandable without noisy decoration.

Validation still belongs to backend.

Avoid overusing asterisks when context makes required state clear, but remain consistent.

---

## 20. Help Text

Use help text when a field has non-obvious consequences.

Examples:

- domain DNS instructions;
- integration override;
- publish behavior;
- Block data binding.

Do not add explanatory text under every obvious field.

---

## 21. Validation Errors

Errors should appear near the relevant field.

Use Russian, user-safe wording.

Do not expose raw backend exception strings.

---

## 22. Dialog Usage

Use Dialog for focused short workflows.

Examples:

- create Workspace;
- create Folder;
- edit small setting;
- copy vehicle.

Do not put a full multi-step complex system inside a tiny Dialog.

---

## 23. Sheet / Drawer Usage

Use Sheet/Drawer for contextual side editing where appropriate.

Examples:

- quick Site details;
- filters on smaller screens;
- secondary configuration.

Designer Properties Panel is a persistent product panel, not necessarily a generic Sheet.

---

## 24. AlertDialog

Use AlertDialog for consequential confirmation.

Examples:

- delete;
- remove;
- disconnect;
- discard destructive change.

Do not use AlertDialog for routine Save.

---

## 25. Cards

Cards should represent meaningful grouped entities.

Examples:

- Site;
- Template;
- Integration Profile;
- Plan;
- Marketplace item.

Avoid wrapping every small section in nested Cards.

Too many nested borders reduce hierarchy.

---

## 26. Site Cards

Site Card should prioritize:

- Site identity;
- preview/thumbnail;
- status;
- domain/subdomain;
- last activity;
- primary action/menu.

Do not overload the card with every Site metric.

---

## 27. Template Cards

Template card should show enough to choose:

- preview;
- title;
- category;
- free/paid later;
- author where relevant.

Selection UX should make the chosen Template obvious.

---

## 28. Tables

Use Table for dense structured data.

Examples:

- submissions;
- Workspace members;
- domains;
- integration deliveries;
- catalog records.

Do not force visual cards where comparison across columns matters.

---

## 29. Table Actions

Row actions should normally be grouped into:

- direct high-frequency action;
- overflow menu for secondary/destructive actions.

Avoid a row containing 8 visible icon buttons.

---

## 30. Table Empty State

Never show an empty bordered table with no explanation.

Use:

- clear empty message;
- optional action.

Example:

`Заявок пока нет.`

---

## 31. Table Loading

Use Skeleton or clear loading state.

Avoid sudden layout shift where practical.

---

## 32. Status Badges

Use consistent status colors/labels.

Examples:

```text
Черновик
Опубликован
Неактивен
Ошибка
Ожидает
Доставлено
```

Internal enum names stay English.

Centralize mappings.

---

## 33. Badges Are Not Buttons

Do not make Badge clickable unless semantics are very clear.

Use buttons/toggles for interaction.

---

## 34. Empty States

Empty states should answer:

1. What is empty?
2. Why might it matter?
3. What can the user do next?

Example:

```text
Сайтов пока нет
Создайте первый сайт и выберите шаблон.
[Создать сайт]
```

---

## 35. Onboarding

Onboarding should be concise.

Do not create a long tutorial before the user can interact with the product.

Prefer contextual guidance at the moment it is needed.

---

## 36. Loading States

Use:

- Skeleton for content placeholders;
- spinner/progress for actions;
- disabled state while submitting.

Do not hide all page content behind a generic spinner unnecessarily.

---

## 37. Long-Running Actions

For operations such as:

- Publish;
- import;
- integration test;
- media processing;

show clear state.

Examples:

```text
Проверяем подключение…
Публикуем сайт…
Импортируем автомобили…
```

---

## 38. Success Feedback

Routine success may use Toast.

Examples:

- `Изменения сохранены`
- `Автомобиль добавлен`

Do not show unnecessary modal confirmation for normal success.

---

## 39. Error Feedback

Use:

- inline error for local field issue;
- banner/alert for page-level failure;
- toast for transient action failure.

Critical persistent failures must not disappear only as a toast.

---

## 40. Icon Policy

Use the project's approved icon library.

Keep icon style consistent.

Do not mix:

- Lucide;
- random SVG packs;
- emoji;
- custom icons

without clear need.

---

## 41. Icon Buttons

Icon-only buttons must have:

- accessible label;
- tooltip where useful.

Do not rely on users guessing unfamiliar icons.

---

## 42. Typography

Use a consistent typography hierarchy.

Typical hierarchy:

- Page title
- Section heading
- Card heading
- Body
- Secondary/muted text
- Caption

Do not use arbitrary font sizes per page.

---

## 43. Line Length

Long explanatory content should have readable width.

Do not stretch paragraphs across very wide monitors.

Designer/canvas surfaces are exceptions.

---

## 44. Muted Text

Use muted text for secondary information.

Do not make critical actions/instructions too low-contrast.

---

## 45. Color Usage

Use semantic design tokens.

Examples:

- primary;
- destructive;
- muted;
- success/status where standardized.

Do not hardcode many arbitrary HEX values inside product components.

---

## 46. Light / Dark Mode

Follow existing project appearance capability.

Do not build a separate theme system.

All new dashboard components should remain legible in supported appearance modes.

Published customer Site theme is a separate Site Design Token system.

---

## 47. Contrast

Maintain reasonable text/control contrast.

Do not use decorative low-contrast text for important information.

---

## 48. Borders

Use borders intentionally for hierarchy.

Avoid:

- Card inside Card inside bordered container inside bordered section.

Prefer spacing/background hierarchy when possible.

---

## 49. Radius

Use consistent radius tokens from the product/shadcn theme.

Do not invent random rounded values for every component.

---

## 50. Shadows

Use shadows sparingly.

Authenticated SaaS UI should not look like a collection of floating marketing cards.

Use subtle elevation when hierarchy requires it.

---

## 51. Spacing

Use a consistent spacing rhythm.

Avoid random values like:

- `mt-[13px]`
- `gap-[17px]`

unless exact design implementation requires it.

Prefer Tailwind spacing scale.

---

## 52. Responsive Design

Every feature should have an intentional behavior on narrower screens.

Possible behaviors:

- stack;
- wrap;
- horizontal scroll;
- collapse secondary actions;
- use Sheet for filters.

Do not simply let layouts overflow.

---

## 53. Desktop-First Designer

Landflow Designer may be desktop-first.

Still define behavior for smaller screens:

- limited mode;
- warning;
- collapsed panels;
- preview-only behavior;

rather than accidental breakage.

Exact product behavior can be decided later.

---

## 54. Published Site Responsive Quality

Customer-facing Blocks must be polished on:

- desktop;
- tablet;
- mobile.

This is a product-critical quality bar.

---

## 55. Designer Layout

Target architecture:

```text
Top Bar
├── Site/Page controls
├── breakpoint controls
├── Preview
└── Publish

Left Panel
├── Pages
├── Blocks
├── Navigator
└── Assets

Canvas
└── Page preview/editor

Right Panel
└── Properties
```

Do not turn the Designer into unrelated dashboard cards.

---

## 56. Designer Panel Density

Designer is a professional tool.

It may be denser than normal settings pages.

Still maintain:

- readable labels;
- clear grouping;
- consistent controls;
- predictable sections.

---

## 57. Properties Panel

Properties should be grouped semantically.

Typical sections:

- Content
- Data
- Layout
- Design
- Actions

Use Russian labels:

- `Контент`
- `Данные`
- `Макет`
- `Дизайн`
- `Действия`

---

## 58. Schema-Driven Controls

Properties Panel control type comes from Block Schema.

Examples:

```text
text → Input
textarea → Textarea
boolean → Switch
select → Select
image → Media picker
group → grouped fields
repeater → repeatable item editor
```

Do not style each Block editor independently.

---

## 59. Repeater UX

Repeater item UI should support:

- reorder handle;
- clear item title;
- duplicate;
- delete;
- collapse/expand where helpful.

Avoid huge always-expanded repeaters when many items exist.

---

## 60. Data Binding UX

When a field is bound to structured data, UI should make source clear.

Example:

```text
Источник: Цена предложения
```

Manual override vs inherited/bound state should eventually be visually distinguishable.

---

## 61. Inherited Values

For Site Design Tokens or fallback values, UI should indicate when value is:

- inherited;
- overridden.

Removing override should restore inherited source.

---

## 62. Automotive UI

Automotive management UI should prioritize structured relationships.

Examples:

- cascading Make → Model → Generation → Trim;
- clear pricing;
- colors/swatches;
- images;
- availability.

Do not make automotive configuration one giant generic JSON form.

---

## 63. Automotive Color Swatches

Color UI must support:

- single color;
- dual/two-tone swatches;
- manufacturer name;
- selected state.

Do not represent every vehicle color with one circle only.

---

## 64. Automotive Images

Transparent vehicle images should render on intentional backgrounds.

Do not accidentally make transparency invisible or introduce ugly checkerboard backgrounds in normal product UI.

Image-management screens may use explicit transparency preview.

---

## 65. Price Editing

Price UI should:

- show currency clearly;
- use consistent number formatting;
- distinguish RRP/current price/benefits;
- avoid ambiguous free-text inputs.

Example display:

`3 890 000 ₽`

---

## 66. Permissions UI

Do not expose technical permission keys directly to normal users.

Use Russian human-readable labels.

Example:

```text
edit_prices
→ Редактирование цен
```

---

## 67. Entitlement UI

When a capability is unavailable due to plan:

- explain briefly;
- distinguish from lack of User permission;
- provide upgrade path later where appropriate.

Do not label every denied action simply `Нет доступа`.

---

## 68. Workspace Switcher

Workspace switcher should clearly display:

- current Workspace;
- available Workspaces;
- create/switch action according to entitlement.

Do not mix Account settings and Workspace management ambiguously.

---

## 69. Navigation

Primary navigation should remain stable.

Do not move core sections between unrelated locations from page to page.

Potential high-level sections later:

- Sites
- Vehicles
- Assets
- Integrations
- Team
- Developer Platform depending access.

Exact IA follows product phase.

---

## 70. Breadcrumbs

Use breadcrumbs for meaningful hierarchy.

Examples:

```text
Сайты / AutoPort / Настройки
```

Avoid breadcrumbs on simple top-level pages where they add noise.

---

## 71. Tabs

Use Tabs for sibling views of one entity.

Examples:

Site Settings:

- Общие
- SEO
- Домены
- Формы
- Интеграции

Do not use tabs as a replacement for entire application navigation.

---

## 72. Filters

Filters should be easy to clear.

If many filters exist:

- group them;
- show active filter state;
- support reset.

Avoid 20 always-visible controls above every table.

---

## 73. Search

Search inputs should use Russian placeholders.

Example:

`Поиск по сайтам`

Use debounce only where it improves behavior.

---

## 74. Dropdown Menus

Use Dropdown Menu for secondary row/entity actions.

Keep destructive actions separated visually.

Example:

```text
Открыть
Дублировать
---
Удалить
```

---

## 75. Tooltips

Tooltips explain unfamiliar icon actions.

Do not hide essential instructions exclusively in tooltip.

---

## 76. Skeletons

Skeletons should approximate final layout.

Do not show arbitrary gray rectangles unrelated to actual component structure.

---

## 77. Progress

Use Progress for measurable progress.

For indeterminate operations, use appropriate loading indication instead of fake percentages.

---

## 78. Data Density

Landflow serves professional users.

Moderate information density is acceptable.

Do not over-space every screen like a marketing landing page.

Balance:

- fast scanning;
- clarity;
- usable touch/keyboard targets.

---

## 79. Marketing vs Product UI

Do not copy marketing-page visual patterns into operational UI indiscriminately.

Dashboard/Designer priorities:

- efficiency;
- hierarchy;
- predictability;
- information density.

---

## 80. Webflow Inspiration

Webflow is a UX/product reference, not a pixel-perfect clone target.

Use useful patterns such as:

- Site dashboard;
- Designer structure;
- property grouping;
- preview/publish mental model.

Do not copy proprietary assets/code/text.

---

## 81. Empty Designer State

If a Page has no Blocks:

show a useful action such as:

```text
На странице пока нет блоков.
[Добавить блок]
```

Do not leave a blank canvas with no guidance.

---

## 82. Empty Vehicle State

Example:

```text
Автомобили ещё не добавлены
Выберите автомобили из каталога Landflow.
[Добавить автомобиль]
```

---

## 83. Empty Form State

Example:

```text
Форм пока нет
Создайте форму для заявок с сайта.
[Создать форму]
```

---

## 84. Empty Submission State

Example:

```text
Заявок пока нет
Новые заявки с опубликованных форм появятся здесь.
```

---

## 85. Publish State

UI should clearly communicate:

- draft;
- unpublished changes;
- publishing;
- published;
- failed.

Do not show only a generic green dot with unclear meaning.

---

## 86. Dangerous Production Actions

Production-affecting actions deserve explicit UI.

Examples:

- Publish;
- change primary domain;
- rollback;
- disconnect Integration.

Do not hide these inside ambiguous generic controls.

---

## 87. Accessibility

Use shadcn/Radix accessibility behavior rather than bypassing it.

Do not strip:

- focus ring;
- aria attributes;
- keyboard handling

without a strong reason.

---

## 88. Focus Ring

Visible keyboard focus is required.

Do not globally remove outlines/focus rings for aesthetics.

---

## 89. Form Labels

Every form control requires an accessible label.

Placeholder is not a replacement.

---

## 90. Image Alt

Meaningful public images need alt-text strategy.

Decorative images may use empty alt where semantically appropriate.

Designer image fields should support alt metadata where needed.

---

## 91. Motion

Use motion sparingly in product UI.

Avoid unnecessary animations that slow routine work.

Respect reduced-motion preferences where relevant, especially in public Blocks.

---

## 92. Hover States

Clickable elements must have clear hover/focus/active states.

Do not make static and interactive rows visually indistinguishable.

---

## 93. Cursor Pointer

Use appropriate cursor semantics.

Do not add `cursor-pointer` to non-interactive elements.

---

## 94. Mobile Touch Targets

On mobile-capable surfaces, avoid tiny icon actions.

Ensure controls remain reasonably tappable.

---

## 95. Browser QA

Important UI changes must be checked in actual browser when Playwright/browser tooling is available.

Review:

- Desktop
- Tablet
- Mobile where relevant
- console
- long Russian strings
- loading
- empty
- error
- permissions

---

## 96. Screenshot Review

Significant pages should be screenshot-reviewed.

Particularly:

- Dashboard;
- Site creation;
- Designer;
- Catalog;
- Publish flow;
- Integration screens.

Do not approve visual quality from JSX alone.

---

## 97. No Arbitrary Visual Drift

If a new feature requires a new visual primitive:

first check whether it can be represented with:

- existing shadcn primitive;
- existing Landflow shared component;
- existing token.

Do not create isolated design language for one screen.

---

## 98. Component Naming

Use English code names.

Examples:

```text
SiteCard
CreateSiteDialog
WorkspaceSwitcher
PropertiesPanel
VehiclePriceEditor
```

Visible labels remain Russian.

---

## 99. UI Task Definition of Done

A UI task is DONE only when applicable checks pass:

```text
TypeScript/check
Production build
Browser interaction
Console clean
Responsive behavior
Russian UI
Empty state
Loading state
Error state
Permission/entitlement state
Accessibility sanity
Visual review
```

---

## 100. Final UI Checklist

Before finishing a Landflow UI task, ask:

```text
Am I using shadcn instead of rebuilding primitives?
Does this screen look like the same product as the rest of Landflow?
Is every user-facing string Russian?
Will longer Russian text fit?
Is there one clear primary action?
Are loading, empty and error states defined?
Are destructive actions explicit?
Are permissions and entitlements represented correctly?
Is keyboard/focus behavior preserved?
Did I verify the actual browser when required?
```

If a critical answer is unclear, the task is not DONE.
